@Zones
Each grid cell of the board belongs to zero or more zones. The zones are:

þ 0-15 ($00-$0F) þ User-defined unordered zones.
!u;Unordered zones

þ 16-31 ($10-$1F) þ User-defined ordered zones.
!o;Ordered zones

þ 32-47 ($20-$2F) þ The element at either the main layer or the under layer
has the class whose number is the zone number minus thirty-two.

þ 48-55 ($30-$37) þ If the corresponding bit of the overlay kind at that
cell is set, where 48-51 are the user-defined flags, 52 is the solid flag,
53 is the reserved flag, 54 is the BG thru flag, and 55 is the visible flag.

þ 56-63 ($38-$3F) þ If user-defined attribute A to H is set for the element
at the main layer or under layer.

þ 64-79 ($40-$4F) þ The element at the main layer has the class whose number
is the zone number minus sixty-four.

þ 80-95 ($50-$5F) þ The element at the main layer if it is a floor, or the
under layer if the main layer is not a floor, has the class whose number is
the zone number minus eighty.

þ 126 ($7E) þ The light shape around the player.

þ 127 ($7F) þ This zone automatically contains all cells in the board grid.

þ 128-255 ($80-$8F) þ Any grid cell is in this zone iff it is not in the
zone with the number of this one minus 128. For example, zone 129 consists
of all cells that are not in zone 1 and vice versa.

(Other numbers are not currently meaningful.)

A stat can be set as restricted to movement within a specified zone.

:u;Unordered zones
Unordered zones consist of only a set of grid cells; each cell either
belongs or does not belong to the zone.

In addition, there are flags, and a 16-bit extra value which you can use for
your own use.

:o;Ordered zones
Ordered zones consist of an ordered sequence of grid cells, so that you
can specify the order of movement along the zone, etc.

An ordered zone has flags and a 16-bit extra value like a unordered zone has
(although the set of available flags is different).

:f;Flags
This section lists the possible flags of zones.

The flags that are applicable to both ordered and unordered zones are:

þ User0 to User3 þ No built-in use; you can use them for your own use.

The flags that are only applicable to ordered zones are:

þ Reverse þ Reverses the effective order of the cells in the zone. This
affects adding new cells to the zone, as well as some operations during
the game which involve the order of cells in the zone.

þ Cycle þ Means that the first cell comes immediately after the last cell
(and the last cell comes immediately before the first cell).

The flags dealing with rotation zones (which are always ordered zones) are:

þ Affect ____ layer þ This tells which layers should be affected. If none
are specified, then this zone is not used as a rotation zone.

A rotation zone also has a movement kind, which is one of:

þ Cyclic þ All cells in the specified layers move in a cycle; the last tile
is moved to the first. All attributes are ignored.

þ Full þ Anything that is not a floor is moved, and floors are moved
between the main layer and under layer.

þ Normal þ Like "Full" but the movement classes are considered, and if it
cannot move on the class of the floor it is moving to then it cannot move.

þ Pushable þ Like "Normal" but only objects that can be pushed in the
direction of movement will be moved.

:c;Comparison of unordered vs ordered zones
Ordered zones are limited to 65535 cells each, but unordered zones can
each contain any number of cells.

Enumeration of cells in a zone, and counting how many cells are in a zone,
is more efficent for ordered zones.

Checking whether or not a cell is in a zone is usually more efficient for
unordered zones, especially for zones containing many cells.

Only ordered zones can be used for rotation (the ZROT instruction).
